  "query_preview": "..This morning's water vapor imagery shows a ridge\nof high pressure over the Four Corners region with a trough over\nBaja California, oriented in a west-east. This set will continue\nthe hot temperatures today, although, it will be slightly\ncooler. But, temperatures will be running above normal. Plus,\nthere is mid-level moisture over the International Border with\nPWATs 0.6 inches up to close to 0.70 inches. This will provide\nweather challenges for today and tomorrow. \n\nToday...Ensemble and hi-res models show the development of\nthicker mid level clouds through the rest of today. There is\nenough instability and moisture to trigger virga and dry \nlightning. The probability of dry lightning is less than 5 \npercent, but still a a risk due to the dry conditions on the \nground. The main threat from virga will be the gusty and erratic\nwinds. The HREF has 30% of seeing gusts greater than 30kts over\na large swath of area from Tucson south and east. A few virga \nshowers can be stronger, resulting in stronger gusts. The bulk \nof the activity will occur this afternoon and diminishing in the\nearly evening. The cloud cover will stick around tonight.\n\nTomorrow...The trough will push north and shift east, bringing\nsouthwesterly to westerly breezes of 15-20 mph with gusts up to\n30 mph across the region. Elevated fire weather concerns will \ndevelop in the afternoon as well. The trough will shove the \nmoisture to the eastern portion of the county warning area with \nCochise, Graham, and Greenlee county with less than 5% for dry \nlightning. Otherwise, another round of virga with gusty winds \nin the afternoon.\n\nThursday and beyond...Our pattern will be zonal flow through the\nstart of the weekend. Westerly winds will develop with elevated\nfire weather conditions south and east of Tucson on Thursday. \nAnother trough will develop by the late weekend over the \nwestern United States. This will be a dry system with stronger \nbreezes. As of now, the latest forecast has Sunday being a\nbreezier day. Temperatures will be near normal and Minor \nHeatRisk by the start of the new week.",
